{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-opg-48767-closing-lemma-for-diffeomorphism-dynam-0dc9bf0d-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T15:37:58Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-opg-48767-closing-lemma-for-diffeomorphism-dynam-0dc9bf0d-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-opg-48767-closing-lemma-for-diffeomorphism-dynam-0dc9bf0d-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-opg-48767-closing-lemma-for-diffeomorphism-dynam-0dc9bf0d-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/OPG-48767"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Conjecture Let $f\\in Diff^{r}(M)$ and $p\\in\\omega_{f}$. Then for any neighborhood $V_{f}\\subset Diff^{r}(M)$ there is $g\\in V_{f}$ such that $p$ is periodic point of $g$ There is an analogous conjecture for flows ( $C^{r}$ vector fields. In the case of diffeos this was proved by Charles Pugh for $r = 1$. In the case of Flows this has been solved by Sushei Hayahshy for $r = 1$. But in the two cases the problem is wide open for $r > 1$"}, "statement_sha256": "215529e223e50c940472ff87f95149bd36a430308aef055596b42d959fd3aaba"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-opg-48767-closing-lemma-for-diffeomorphism-dynam-0dc9bf0d-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/OPG-48767"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256"